<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jalairs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jalayir tribe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jalairs were a Mongol tribe that served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and his successors; his general Muqali was a Jalair. Jalair commanders were prominent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and after its collapse one of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg">Hasan Buzurg</a>, founded the Jalayirid dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the 1330s. Other Jalair groups became part of the Chagatai realm and of the Kazakh Senior Zhuz.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalairs.jpg" alt="Jalairs" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homeland_of_the_Jalair_tribe_of_the_Mongol_Empire_in_1207.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jalairs, a tribe of Mongolic or Turkic origin, may be the Chaladi recorded in Chinese sources of 910 in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. They later lived along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kherlen-river">Kerulen</a> and Orkhon rivers, between the Merkits and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraits</a>. The historian Yury Zuev proposed that their name is a Mongolian form of Yaglakar, the dynastic clan of the Uyghur Khaganate, and some scholars link them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Rashid-al-Din Hamadani, writing in the early 14th century, counted ten large divisions, among them the Jait, Oyat, Kirkin and Turi.</p><p>The Jalairs revolted against Khitan rule in 1014 and were suppressed the next year. Around 1060 Khaidu of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a>, an ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, conquered them and made them hereditary slaves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. They became an important force in the Khamag Mongol confederation in the 12th century and in the rise of Genghis Khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jalairs served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khans</a> as stewards, chief judges, imperial tutors and advisers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> gave 1,000 men under the Jalair Moqe noyan to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> in Turkestan, and others settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. The descendants of Muqali inherited his title and became a mainstay of Confucian influence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> set out to conquer the Abbasid caliphate in 1252, the Jalairs supplied a strong contingent whose commander, Kok-Elege, took part in sieges from 1256 to 1261. In the Il-khanate the Jalair Buqa installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a> in 1284.</p><p>The Jalayirid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg">Hasan Buzurg</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> through puppet khans after the death of the Il-khan Arpa Ke'un in 1336, expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a> in 1432. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> the Jalairs became one of the main clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> and stayed active for two more centuries. In the 16th century they were one of the 14 clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> tumen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Jalairs are one of the founding tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a>, the largest ethnic group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and the Jalaid are a clan and a banner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> they belong to the Senior Jüz of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and are found among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpaks">Karakalpaks</a> and Kyrgyz. Timur brought 400 Jalair families to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, where hereditary Jalayir chiefs held Kalat-i-nadiri until the end of the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jalairs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chagatai Khanate's government was always contested between the Genghisid legitimacy of the Chagataid khans and the practical power of the great tribal groupings, among them the Dughlat in the east and the Barlas, <b>Jalayir</b> and Qara'una in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Jalayirs</b> were a Mongol tribe prominent in the Ilkhanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/jalayirids">Jalayirids</a></li><li><blockquote>Its tribes, such as the Dulat, Uysun and <b>Jalayir</b>, were nomadic herders closely tied to the oasis cities of Tashkent and Turkistan.</blockquote><a href="/state/senior-juz">Senior Juz</a></li><li><blockquote>Real power lay with the Turkic and Mongol tribal amirs of the Barlas, <b>Jalayir</b>, Arlat and Qara'unas, who had settled among the towns of Transoxiana and adopted Islam.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-chagatai-khanate">Western Chagatai Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
